.TH FERNKBD 1 "October 2026" "fernkbd" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fernkbd \- inject terminal input as virtual keyboard events via uinput
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fernkbd
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fernkbd
reads keystrokes from the terminal and injects them as global Linux input
events through
.IR /dev/uinput .
Anything typed into the terminal appears as if it came from a physical
keyboard. This is useful when working over SSH and you need to type into a
local (or remote-desktop) session.
.PP
The program puts the terminal into raw mode. A status line shows the last
key that was injected. Press
.B Ctrl+C
three times in a row to exit; a single or double Ctrl+C is forwarded to the
target application.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version information and exit.
.SH KEYS
Supported input includes:
.IP \(bu 2
US-layout letters, digits, and common punctuation (with Shift)
.IP \(bu 2
Ctrl+A through Ctrl+Z and related control characters
.IP \(bu 2
Arrow keys, Home, End, Insert, Delete, PageUp, PageDown
.IP \(bu 2
F1–F12
.IP \(bu 2
Alt+character (ESC followed by a character)
.PP
Unmapped sequences are ignored.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B fernkbd
sees only what the terminal encodes as a byte stream, not native hardware
key events. That imposes several constraints:
.TP
.B Character stream, not key events
The program receives characters and escape sequences, not independent
key-down / key-up reports. Modifier keys held alone (e.g. pressing Ctrl
without another key) are not visible and cannot be injected as held
modifiers.
.TP
.B Ambiguous keys
Some keys are indistinguishable in a terminal. Tab and Ctrl+I produce the
same byte; Enter and Ctrl+M likewise. The mapping prefers the common
interpretation (Tab, Enter).
.TP
.B Limited chords
Only combinations that terminals traditionally encode are available
(Ctrl+letter, Alt+character via an ESC prefix, Shift via uppercase or
shifted punctuation). Arbitrary multi-modifier chords (e.g. Ctrl+Alt+Shift+F5)
are generally not transmitted by the terminal and therefore cannot be
injected.
.TP
.B Escape-sequence coverage
Arrow keys, function keys, and navigation keys are recognised for common
CSI and SS3 forms. Sequences that include modifier parameters
(e.g. Ctrl+Up as CSI with a parameter) are not fully parsed yet and may
be ignored or partially matched. Behaviour can also depend on the
terminal's cursor-key mode (normal vs application).
.TP
.B Layout
Character-to-keycode mapping assumes a US QWERTY layout. Other layouts
are not translated.
.TP
.B No mouse
Terminal mouse protocols report character-cell coordinates only, not a
full pointer device.
.B fernkbd
does not enable or inject mouse events; an X11 or similar client path is
a better fit for pointer input.
.TP
.B Exit sequence
Because a single Ctrl+C must be injectable, quitting requires three
consecutive Ctrl+C presses.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Linux with
.I /dev/uinput
.IP \(bu 2
Permission to write to
.I /dev/uinput
(root, or membership in the
.B input
group)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including after a normal triple-Ctrl+C exit).
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command-line option or fatal error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uinput (4),
.BR evdev (4)
